  "id": "COMPOUND:1081 - DISEASE:2183",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a potential mechanism by which capecitabine may contribute to the development of neck pain, through a series of interconnected biological pathways. The drug is linked to macrophage stimulating 1, which is associated with Haemangiopericytoma and Diabetic Angiopathies, both of which are linked to neck pain. This indicates that capecitabine may have a role in the pathogenesis of neck pain through the modulation of macrophage activity. Additionally, the drug is associated with the CF transmembrane conductance regulator, which is linked to Aspergillosis and neck pain, suggesting a broader involvement in inflammatory and vascular processes. The consistent presence of these pathways, despite varying scores, implies that capecitabine may influence cellular processes that contribute to the development of neck pain. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between capecitabine and neck pain, though further research is needed to confirm the direct relationship and to understand the precise role of these pathways in the condition.",
